        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying Too Small a Trimmer for the Yard</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A light 20-volt trimmer is great on a townhouse lot and miserable on a half-acre with fence lines. If you finish every session with a dead battery and half the job left, the tool is undersized. Match swath width and voltage to the property, or plan on a second battery from the start.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using the Wrong Line Diameter</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Forcing thick line into a head built for thinner line overloads the motor and jams the spool, while line that is too thin breaks constantly. The correct diameter is printed on the head or in the manual. Stick to it, and replace old brittle line rather than fighting it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trimming Without Eye Protection</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A spinning line throws stones and debris at high speed, and eye injuries from trimmers are common. Wear impact-rated safety glasses or goggles every time, no matter how small the job. Add hearing protection with a gas trimmer and long pants for leg protection. Ordinary prescription glasses are not a substitute.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Scalping Trees and Fence Posts</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Running the line against a tree trunk strips bark and can kill young trees over time. Hitting posts and siding shreds line and leaves marks. Mulch rings around trees and slow, careful passes near structures save the tree, the line and your time. Trim slowly near anything you do not want damaged.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Storing Gas Trimmers With Old Fuel</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Fuel left in a carburetor over winter turns to varnish and blocks the jets, and that is the top reason gas trimmers will not start in spring. Use stabilizer or ethanol-free canned fuel, or run the tank dry before storage. A carburetor cleaning at a shop often costs as much as a new budget trimmer.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are weed eaters and string trimmers the same thing?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Weed Eater was originally a trademark, and the name stuck as the everyday word for a string trimmer, the same way people say weed whacker. All three describe a tool that spins nylon line to cut grass and weeds where a mower cannot reach. Brush cutters are a related category with metal blades, heavier construction and handlebars for clearing woody growth.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is battery or gas better for a weed eater?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For yards up to about half an acre, a quality battery trimmer is the better choice for most people: it starts instantly, is quieter, and needs little maintenance. Gas makes more sense for large or rural properties, regular brush clearing, and anyone who needs to run for hours. A second battery closes much of the runtime gap for mid-size yards.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does my trimmer line keep breaking?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Common causes are old, dried-out line, the wrong diameter for the head, hitting concrete, posts or fences with the line, and running at full speed into hard edges. Replace old line, use the size listed for your head, approach hard surfaces at an angle, and let the tip of the line do the cutting rather than burying the head in growth.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many minutes does a cordless trimmer last per charge?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the voltage, battery capacity, speed setting and how thick the growth is. A mid-size battery on a brushless trimmer typically handles a suburban trim and edge on one charge, while thick, wet grass on high speed can cut runtime sharply. Box claims assume light conditions, so a second battery is the practical solution for larger yards.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I edge with a string trimmer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Rotate the trimmer so the head spins vertically, line it up with the edge of the driveway or walk, and walk slowly. Many models have a wheel guide or a rotating head made for edging. Expect heavy line wear against concrete. For long driveways edged weekly, a dedicated edger is faster and cleaner.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size string trimmer do I need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A 12-inch swath suits small, well-kept yards. Thirteen to 15 inches suits most suburban properties. Sixteen inches or more and a higher-voltage battery or gas engine suits large lots and long fence lines. Match swath to power; a wide swath on a weak motor drains batteries and bogs down in heavy growth.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a string trimmer cut brush?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only light brush and weeds. Nylon line is not designed for woody stems, saplings or thick vines. For that work you need a brush cutter or a trimmer the manufacturer rates for a metal blade, used with the specified guard, handle and harness. Using a blade on a trimmer not built for it is dangerous because of kickback and thrown debris.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

